// Conditional compilation — `#if` / `#else` / `#endif`, resolved at the TOKEN TIER.
//
// ⭐⭐ **THIS IS NOT WHERE EITHER REFERENCE PUTS IT, AND THE DIVERGENCE IS FORCED BY shv2'S OWN
// STRUCTURE.** Both references resolve directives inside the PARSER — v1 dispatches every body loop
// through `Parser.tryHandleDirective` (`maxon-selfhosted/Compiler/Parser.maxon:17760`, called from
// seven loops at `:615/2388/5233/5768/5975/6565/6829`), and the bootstrap parses to an AST first.
// **shv2 cannot do that, because shv2 reads a file's TOKENS TWICE.**
//
// `queryProgramSignatures` runs a whole-program DECLARATION SWEEP over the raw token array
// (`Parser.foldDeclaredSignaturesInto`) before any file is parsed, and that sweep is not the parser's
// walk — it is its own linear scan with its own block-depth counter and four sub-walks of its own
// (`recordScannedType`, `recordScannedSignature`, `recordScannedEnum`, `recordScannedInterface`).
// Neither reference has such a pass; v1 has no signature index at all. Teaching directives to the
// PARSE loops alone would leave the SWEEP reading both branches of every `#if`, and the two would
// disagree about which declarations the file contains:
//
//   • **MEASURED, on the very file this mechanism exists to unblock.** `stdlib/FilePath.maxon:3-11`
//     declares `pathSeparator` in BOTH arms of a top-level `#if os(Windows)`/`#else`. The sweep is
//     FIRST-WINS (`ProgramSignatures.recordDecl`), so it would record the Windows arm's line 4 on
//     every target; on Linux/macOS/wasi the parse dispatches the `#else` arm at line 8, and
//     `isRedeclaredBinding` compares those positions and reports **E3006 duplicate definition** — a
//     correct program rejected on three targets out of four. Reverse the arms and it is worse: the
//     parse binds the name to the OTHER branch's value, silently.
//   • A `#if`-gated FIELD PAIR in a `type` body is worse still, and silent everywhere:
//     `recordScannedType` records fields into the whole-program `StructLayout`, so recording both
//     arms lays every later field at the wrong offset with no diagnostic at all.
//
// ⇒ **The directives are resolved ONCE, HERE, before anything reads the tokens.** `queryActiveTokens`
// layers this filter over `queryTokens`, and the sweep and the parse are handed the SAME filtered
// array — so they cannot come to disagree about which tokens exist. That is a structural guarantee,
// not a thing to verify at each call site, and it reaches the walks a per-loop arm would still have
// missed: `scanDescriptorNeedsInto`, the lazy sibling-receiver scan, `buildBlockExtentIndex`,
// `assignedBindingsIn`, `fileNamesStringIndexProducer`, and the constant evaluator's transient
// `Parser` over `decl.tokens` (which IS the declaring file's array, so it inherits the filtering).
//
// **What is NOT a divergence is the SEMANTICS, and it is measured on the bootstrap oracle:**
//
//   • lexical garbage in a dead branch → `E1002 Unterminated string literal` ⇒ the dead branch IS
//     lexed. So the whole file lexes first; this pass runs over finished tokens.
//   • an unresolved call in a dead branch → compiles ⇒ it is never parsed or resolved.
//   • a duplicate `static function` in a dead `#else` of a type body → compiles ⇒ same at declaration
//     level.
//
// A token-level skip with a depth counter is exactly that behaviour, and it is what both references
// implement. One linear pass, O(1) state per open region, no allocation beyond the output array.

// The directive TokenKinds are emitted by the lexer (`Lexer.scanDirective`) and enumerated ONCE here,
// by `isDirectiveToken`; an unrecognized `#name` never reaches this pass at all — it is
// `E1009 lexerUnknownDirective`, a lexer error.

// A structural or semantic fault in a directive region. Surfaced by `Queries.reportDirectiveError`,
// which is the only caller — the filter runs inside a query, where `project` is in scope, so a
// throwing union keeps the diagnostic wording in the same file as every other query-level report.
export union DirectiveError implements Error
	// A `#if` still open at end of file. Positioned at the `#if` that has no `#endif` — the
	// INNERMOST open one, which is the one whose partner is missing however many enclose it.
	unterminatedIf(line LineNumber, column ColumnNumber)
	// An `#else` or an `#endif` with no open `#if`. Carries the offending spelling so the message
	// can name what it actually saw rather than enumerating both.
	unmatchedDirective(spelling String, line LineNumber, column ColumnNumber)
	// A condition calls a predicate that is not one of the five. See `predicateNamed`.
	unknownPredicate(name String, line LineNumber, column ColumnNumber)
	// A condition ran out of tokens, or holds something the grammar has no production for — a
	// missing `)`, an operator with no right-hand side, a bare identifier with no argument list.
	malformedCondition(line LineNumber, column ColumnNumber)
	// A condition that PARSES, followed by more tokens on the same line. Its own case rather than a
	// second use of `malformedCondition`, because the advice differs — there is nothing to correct
	// in the condition itself — and because it is the one directive fault whose absence was a silent
	// WRONG ANSWER rather than a poor message. See `expectConditionEnd`.
	trailingConditionTokens(line LineNumber, column ColumnNumber)
end 'DirectiveError'

// One open `#if`. `branchLive` already folds in the enclosing region's liveness (see
// `pushConditional`), so "is the cursor in live code?" is a read of the innermost frame alone and
// never a walk of the stack.
type DirectiveFrame
	// Is the branch currently being walked emitted?
	export var branchLive as bool
	// Has ANY arm of this `#if` been taken? What makes `#else` the complement of `#if` rather than
	// a second unconditional arm. Distinct from `not branchLive`, which is also true while the
	// `#if` arm is being skipped and its `#else` has not yet been reached.
	//
	// ⚠ It answers ONLY that question, and stays `false` for an `#if` inside a dead region even
	// though no arm of one can ever run — because "this region is unreachable" is a different fact
	// with its own home (`enclosingRegionIsLive`), and encoding it here as well would be the same
	// rule in two places.
	export var takenAlready as bool
	// The `#if`'s own position, for the unterminated-region diagnostic.
	export let line as LineNumber
	export let column as ColumnNumber

	export static function create(branchLive bool, takenAlready bool, line LineNumber, column ColumnNumber) returns DirectiveFrame
		return Self{branchLive: branchLive, takenAlready: takenAlready, line: line, column: column}
	end 'create'
end 'DirectiveFrame'

typealias DirectiveFrameArray = Array with DirectiveFrame

// ⭐ **THE PREDICATES A `#if` CONDITION MAY CALL — THE ENUM *IS* THE ACCEPTED SET.**
//
// It had been two lists: five byte constants the evaluator compared against, and a hand-written
// English sentence the diagnostic printed. They agreed, and nothing made them — a sixth predicate
// could be accepted without being advertised, or advertised without being accepted, and neither
// direction is a compile error. Now `predicateNamed` resolves a spelling by walking `allCases` and
// `acceptedPredicateList` renders the same walk, so acceptance and advertisement are one fact. A new
// case forces an arm in `predicateSpelling` and in `satisfyingArgument` (both matches are exhaustive
// over this enum) and needs no other edit anywhere.
enum ConditionPredicate
	os
	arch
	testing
	rcSanitize
	leakReport
end 'ConditionPredicate'

// The spelling `flag(false)` must carry for an absent build flag to satisfy it. See
// `satisfyingArgument`.
let FalseSpelling = b"false"

// ⭐ **THE FILTER: `tokens` minus every directive token and every inactive branch.**
//
// Positions are untouched — a `Token` carries its own line/column/byte span, so dropping tokens
// cannot shift a diagnostic. What the parser receives is a stream that simply never contained the
// dead code, which is why no downstream walk needs to know this pass exists.
//
// Cost is one pass over the tokens plus one output array. The frame stack is O(nesting depth), which
// is the source's own bracket depth and not a function of file size.
export function filterConditionalTokens(tokens TokenArray, target Target) returns TokenArray throws DirectiveError
	// ⭐ **THE COMMON CASE IS A FILE WITH NO DIRECTIVES AT ALL, AND IT MUST NOT PAY FOR A COPY.**
	// Every file in the scale corpus and all but four in `stdlib/` are in it. The probe is one linear
	// scan of a scalar field — cheaper than the array it would otherwise build, and it returns the
	// memoized token array itself rather than a duplicate of it (`queryTokens` retains it for the
	// whole build regardless, so sharing costs nothing and copying would double the token memory).
	if not tokensHoldDirective(tokens) 'noDirectives'
		return tokens
	end 'noDirectives'

	var out = TokenArray.create()
	var frames = DirectiveFrameArray.create()
	var scan = 0 as TokenIndex

	while scan < tokens.count() 'walk'
		let tok = try tokens.get(scan) otherwise panic("filterConditionalTokens: scan<count just checked but tokens.get({scan}) failed")
		// Read ONCE per token, before any arm can push or pop: for an ordinary token this decides
		// whether it survives, and for a `#if` it is the ENCLOSING region's liveness, which is what
		// the new frame's own liveness is conjoined with.
		let regionLive = regionIsLive(frames)

		if tok.kind == TokenKind.hashIf 'openRegion'
			// ⚠ **THE CONDITION IS EVALUATED ONLY IN LIVE CODE**, and that is not an optimization —
			// it is what makes a nested `#if` inside a dead branch mean nothing. Both references get
			// the same property by token-walking the dead branch without interpreting it; here the
			// dead region's condition tokens simply fall through the `not live` drop below, which is
			// the same answer reached by the rule that was already there.
			if regionLive 'liveParent'
				let cursor = ConditionCursor.create((scan + 1) as TokenIndex)
				let taken = try evaluateCondition(tokens, cursor: cursor, target: target)
				try expectConditionEnd(tokens, cursor: cursor)
				frames.push(DirectiveFrame.create(taken, takenAlready: taken, line: tok.line, column: tok.column))
				scan = cursor.value
				continue
			end 'liveParent'

			// `takenAlready: false` is the HONEST value and not an oversight: no arm of an unreachable
			// `#if` is ever taken. Keeping the `#else` dead is `enclosingRegionIsLive`'s job alone —
			// setting this true would ALSO do it, and having both is one fact written down twice.
			// MEASURED: with both in place, disabling either one on its own broke no test in the
			// suite, which is exactly what a redundant guard looks like. See
			// `directives.else-inside-dead-branch-stays-dead`, the case that now pins the survivor.
			frames.push(DirectiveFrame.create(false, takenAlready: false, line: tok.line, column: tok.column))
			scan = scan + 1
			continue
		end 'openRegion'

		if tok.kind == TokenKind.hashElse 'flipRegion'
			if frames.isEmpty() 'elseWithoutIf'
				throw DirectiveError.unmatchedDirective(TokenKind.hashElse.rawValue, line: tok.line, column: tok.column)
			end 'elseWithoutIf'
			// The ENCLOSING region's liveness, read from the frame below this one — this frame's own
			// `branchLive` describes the arm that just ended and says nothing about whether the `#if`
			// was reachable at all. Without it, an `#else` inside a dead outer branch goes live.
			let enclosingLive = enclosingRegionIsLive(frames)
			var frame = innermostFrame(frames)
			frame.branchLive = enclosingLive and not frame.takenAlready
			frame.takenAlready = frame.takenAlready or frame.branchLive
			scan = scan + 1
			continue
		end 'flipRegion'

		if tok.kind == TokenKind.hashEndif 'closeRegion'
			if frames.isEmpty() 'endifWithoutIf'
				throw DirectiveError.unmatchedDirective(TokenKind.hashEndif.rawValue, line: tok.line, column: tok.column)
			end 'endifWithoutIf'
			_ = try frames.pop() otherwise panic("filterConditionalTokens: frames non-empty just checked but pop() failed")
			scan = scan + 1
			continue
		end 'closeRegion'

		// ⚠ **THE THREE ARMS ABOVE MUST COVER EVERY KIND `isDirectiveToken` ADMITS**, because that
		// predicate is the early-out probe's entire definition of "this file needs filtering". A
		// fourth directive kind added to it and to the lexer but not to a handler here would fall
		// through to the `keep` below and be EMITTED INTO THE PROGRAM — and a fourth kind added to a
		// handler but not to the probe would make a file that uses only that kind skip the filter
		// altogether. One list, checked from both ends, so neither drift is silent.
		if isDirectiveToken(tok.kind) 'unhandledDirective'
			panic("filterConditionalTokens: directive token '{tok.kind.rawValue}' has no handling arm")
		end 'unhandledDirective'

		if regionLive 'keep'
			out.push(tok)
		end 'keep'
		scan = scan + 1
	end 'walk'

	// ⚠ **DIVERGENCE FROM BOTH REFERENCES (E2063): an unterminated region is REFUSED.** v1 returns
	// quietly at EOF (`skipConditionalBranch`, `:17823-17825`) and the bootstrap compiles such a file
	// clean — measured. Accepting it silently SWALLOWS every declaration after the `#if`, and the
	// only symptom is an unrelated "undefined" somewhere else entirely. Verified safe before it was
	// added: all 100 directive lines in `specs/`, `specs-shv2/` and `stdlib/` are balanced.
	//
	// The INNERMOST open region is the one named: its `#endif` is the missing one however many `#if`s
	// enclose it.
	if not frames.isEmpty() 'unterminated'
		let open = innermostFrame(frames)
		throw DirectiveError.unterminatedIf(open.line, column: open.column)
	end 'unterminated'

	return out
end 'filterConditionalTokens'

// The innermost open `#if`'s frame. THE one place the stack top is read, so `regionIsLive`, the
// `#else` arm and the unterminated report cannot come to disagree about which frame is current.
// Callers test `isEmpty` first; a caller that did not would rather see this panic than silently read
// a frame belonging to an enclosing region.
function innermostFrame(frames DirectiveFrameArray) returns DirectiveFrame
	return try frames.get(frames.count() - 1) otherwise panic("innermostFrame: called on an empty directive frame stack")
end 'innermostFrame'

// THE set of token kinds this pass owns, written down once. Read by the early-out probe below and
// asserted against the filter's handler arms (see the `unhandledDirective` panic), so the two cannot
// come to disagree about what a directive IS.
function isDirectiveToken(kind TokenKind) returns bool
	return kind == TokenKind.hashIf or kind == TokenKind.hashElse or kind == TokenKind.hashEndif
end 'isDirectiveToken'

// Does this file contain a directive at all? The gate on `filterConditionalTokens`'s whole body —
// see there for why a directive-free file must not pay for a copy of its token array.
function tokensHoldDirective(tokens TokenArray) returns bool
	for tok in tokens 'each'
		if isDirectiveToken(tok.kind) 'found'
			return true
		end 'found'
	end 'each'
	return false
end 'tokensHoldDirective'

// Is the cursor in code that is emitted? True at the top level (no open `#if`), otherwise the
// innermost frame's own answer — which already folds in every enclosing region (see
// `DirectiveFrame.branchLive`).
function regionIsLive(frames DirectiveFrameArray) returns bool
	if frames.isEmpty() 'topLevel'
		return true
	end 'topLevel'
	return innermostFrame(frames).branchLive
end 'regionIsLive'

// Is the region ENCLOSING the innermost open `#if` live? What an `#else` needs, and what
// `regionIsLive` structurally cannot answer: the innermost frame describes the arm that just ended.
// True when the innermost `#if` is itself at the top level.
function enclosingRegionIsLive(frames DirectiveFrameArray) returns bool
	if frames.count() < 2 'innermostIsTopLevel'
		return true
	end 'innermostIsTopLevel'
	let parent = try frames.get(frames.count() - 2) otherwise panic("enclosingRegionIsLive: count>=2 just checked but get() failed")
	return parent.branchLive
end 'enclosingRegionIsLive'

// A mutable index into the token array, threaded through the condition grammar below.
//
// A `type` rather than an `inout` position, because the grammar is RECURSIVE (`not not os(X)`,
// `(a or b) and c`) and every production must resume where the one it called stopped. Returning the
// index alongside the value from each of five productions would put the same two-field tuple in
// five signatures and make each caller re-thread it; one shared cursor is the fact written down once.
type ConditionCursor
	export var value as TokenIndex

	export static function create(value TokenIndex) returns ConditionCursor
		return Self{value: value}
	end 'create'
end 'ConditionCursor'

// ⭐ **THE CONDITION GRAMMAR — mirrored from v1 (`maxon-selfhosted/Compiler/Parser.maxon:17846-17947`),
// which mirrors it from the bootstrap:**
//
//     condition = or
//     or        = and ('or' and)*
//     and       = unary ('and' unary)*
//     unary     = 'not' unary | atom
//     atom      = '(' condition ')' | IDENT '(' (IDENT | true | false) ')'
//
// Precedence low-to-high is `or`, `and`, `not`; parentheses override at atom level.
//
// ⚠ **NO SHORT-CIRCUIT, DELIBERATELY.** Both operands of every `and`/`or` are always parsed, so the
// cursor lands in the same place whatever the operands evaluate to. A short-circuiting evaluator
// would leave the cursor mid-condition on exactly the inputs that took the fast path, and the filter
// would resume its walk inside the condition's own tokens — emitting `os ( Linux )` into the program.
// A condition has at most a handful of atoms and each is a scalar compare, so there is nothing to win.
function evaluateCondition(tokens TokenArray, cursor ConditionCursor, target Target) returns bool throws DirectiveError
	return try evaluateOr(tokens, cursor: cursor, target: target)
end 'evaluateCondition'

// ⭐ **A CONDITION ENDS AT THE END OF ITS OWN LINE, AND NOTHING MAY FOLLOW IT THERE.**
//
// ⚠ Without this the grammar simply stops at the first token no production wants, and the FILTER
// RESUMES ITS WALK FROM EXACTLY THERE — so text trailing a condition is emitted into the program as
// code. `evaluateCondition`'s no-short-circuit note names that hazard for the evaluator's own
// tokens; this is the same hazard reached from the SOURCE side, and it was live.
//
// It is worth a refusal rather than a shrug because the symptom is TARGET-DEPENDENT. Measured, before
// this existed: `#if os(Windows) os(Linux)` left `os ( Linux )` in the stream and failed with an
// E2015 about a top-level identifier on Windows — and compiled clean on a target where the region is
// dead and the junk is skipped along with it. The same file, accepted or rejected according to who
// is building it, and neither answer mentioning a directive.
//
// The bootstrap oracle is silent here because it discards the remainder of the `#if` line: measured,
// `#if os(Windows) n = 5` assigns nothing there (its `n` stays `let`-eligible) and assigned 5 here.
// Refusing is the only reading that cannot silently mean two different programs. Nothing in the
// corpus is affected — all 100 directive lines in `specs/`, `specs-shv2/` and `stdlib/` end at their
// condition.
//
// `newline` is a real token in this lexer, so "the rest of the line" is one lookahead and no
// position arithmetic. EOF counts as an ending: a `#if` on an unterminated final line is a region
// fault (E2063), reported below with the position of the `#if` itself, not a condition fault.
//
// ⛔ **A `///` DOC COMMENT ENDS THE LINE TOO, AND LEAVING IT OUT WOULD REFUSE SOURCE THAT BUILDS TODAY.**
// `#if os(Windows) /// note` lexes as a `docComment` followed by the line's `newline`; before `///` became
// a token the `//` path swallowed it and the condition ended cleanly. Read as a trailing token it would
// be a NEW E2063 on an unchanged file — and the corpus contains no such line, so nothing would catch it.
// The set is `tokenKindIsSkippedLayout`'s plus EOF, which is the same "nothing the grammar reads follows"
// the parser's own line-end rules mean.
function expectConditionEnd(tokens TokenArray, cursor ConditionCursor) throws DirectiveError
	let tok = tokenAtOrEof(tokens, index: cursor.value)
	if not tokenKindIsSkippedLayout(tok.kind) and tok.kind != TokenKind.eof 'trailing'
		throw DirectiveError.trailingConditionTokens(tok.line, column: tok.column)
	end 'trailing'
end 'expectConditionEnd'

function evaluateOr(tokens TokenArray, cursor ConditionCursor, target Target) returns bool throws DirectiveError
	var result = try evaluateAnd(tokens, cursor: cursor, target: target)
	while acceptKind(tokens, cursor: cursor, kind: TokenKind.or) 'orLoop'
		let rhs = try evaluateAnd(tokens, cursor: cursor, target: target)
		result = result or rhs
	end 'orLoop'
	return result
end 'evaluateOr'

function evaluateAnd(tokens TokenArray, cursor ConditionCursor, target Target) returns bool throws DirectiveError
	var result = try evaluateUnary(tokens, cursor: cursor, target: target)
	while acceptKind(tokens, cursor: cursor, kind: TokenKind.and) 'andLoop'
		let rhs = try evaluateUnary(tokens, cursor: cursor, target: target)
		result = result and rhs
	end 'andLoop'
	return result
end 'evaluateAnd'

function evaluateUnary(tokens TokenArray, cursor ConditionCursor, target Target) returns bool throws DirectiveError
	if acceptKind(tokens, cursor: cursor, kind: TokenKind.not) 'isNot'
		let inner = try evaluateUnary(tokens, cursor: cursor, target: target)
		return not inner
	end 'isNot'
	return try evaluateAtom(tokens, cursor: cursor, target: target)
end 'evaluateUnary'

function evaluateAtom(tokens TokenArray, cursor ConditionCursor, target Target) returns bool throws DirectiveError
	if acceptKind(tokens, cursor: cursor, kind: TokenKind.leftParen) 'isParen'
		let inner = try evaluateCondition(tokens, cursor: cursor, target: target)
		try expectKind(tokens, cursor: cursor, kind: TokenKind.rightParen)
		return inner
	end 'isParen'

	let nameTok = try expectKind(tokens, cursor: cursor, kind: TokenKind.identifier)
	try expectKind(tokens, cursor: cursor, kind: TokenKind.leftParen)
	// The argument is an identifier (`Windows`, `x64`) or a bool keyword (`true`, `false`), which
	// the lexer gives distinct kinds. Its TEXT is what every predicate reads, and `true`/`false`
	// carry their own spelling in `value` — so one read serves all five.
	let argTok = try readConditionArgument(tokens, cursor: cursor)
	try expectKind(tokens, cursor: cursor, kind: TokenKind.rightParen)

	// Resolved AFTER the argument list is consumed, so an unknown name reports its own position while
	// the cursor still lands past the atom — the same order the two lists version had, and what keeps
	// `error.unknown-predicate`'s golden pointing at the name rather than at the `(`.
	let predicate = try predicateNamed(nameTok)
	return equalsIgnoringAsciiCase(argTok.value, b: satisfyingArgument(predicate, target: target))
end 'evaluateAtom'

// The predicate a source spelling names, or E2064. Matched EXACTLY, unlike a predicate's ARGUMENT:
// `os` is a function name in this grammar and the corpus spells all five in camelCase, whereas
// `os(Windows)` names a platform whose conventional spelling is capitalized.
function predicateNamed(nameTok Token) returns ConditionPredicate throws DirectiveError
	for predicate in ConditionPredicate.allCases 'each'
		if nameTok.value.equals(predicateSpelling(predicate)) 'found'
			return predicate
		end 'found'
	end 'each'

	throw DirectiveError.unknownPredicate(String.from(nameTok.value), line: nameTok.line, column: nameTok.column)
end 'predicateNamed'

// Each predicate's source spelling. THE names — read by the resolver above and by the diagnostic
// below, never written down a second time.
function predicateSpelling(predicate ConditionPredicate) returns ByteArray
	return match predicate 'spelling'
		os gives b"os"
		arch gives b"arch"
		testing gives b"testing"
		rcSanitize gives b"rcSanitize"
		leakReport gives b"leakReport"
	end 'spelling'
end 'predicateSpelling'

// ⭐ **WHAT A PREDICATE'S ARGUMENT MUST SPELL FOR IT TO BE TRUE — one comparison serves all five,
// because all five ask the same shape of question: "is your argument the current value of this
// fact?"** So the match yields a VALUE and never a second evaluator, and there is exactly one place
// where an argument is compared to anything.
//
// `os` and `arch` answer out of `Target.maxon`'s canonical names, which is also what `--target=`
// parses through — the platform spellings are that file's fact, not this one's. An UNRECOGNIZED
// argument is therefore FALSE rather than an error, matching both references (and measured on the
// oracle: `os(Plan9)` compiles and takes the `#else`), which is what lets portable source name a
// platform this compiler does not target without becoming unbuildable.
//
// ⭐ **`testing`, `rcSanitize` AND `leakReport` NAME BUILD FLAGS shv2 DOES NOT HAVE — all three are
// OFF, so each is satisfied exactly by `flag(false)`.** That is the correct answer and not a
// stand-in for one. `testing` asks whether the compiler is running under the spec harness: shv2's
// harness SPAWNS the compiler as a subprocess and threads no such flag, so there is nothing for it
// to be true of — and v1 answers the same way outside its own harness
// (`maxon-selfhosted/Compiler/Parser.maxon:17912-17919`), as does the bootstrap with `_testing`
// unset (oracle-measured: exit 2). `rcSanitize` and `leakReport` gate v1's optional refcount
// sanitizer and leak census, neither of which shv2 has; its leak check is unconditional.
//
// One rule for all three, deliberately: v1 spells them separately and its two spellings disagree on
// a NON-BOOLEAN argument (`testing(wibble)` is false there, while `rcSanitize(wibble)` is TRUE — it
// tests only for `"true"` and negates). Uniform here, and in the conservative direction: a nonsense
// argument never switches code ON.
function satisfyingArgument(predicate ConditionPredicate, target Target) returns ByteArray
	return match predicate 'satisfying'
		os gives osCanonicalName(target.os)
		arch gives cpuCanonicalName(target.cpu)
		testing gives FalseSpelling
		rcSanitize gives FalseSpelling
		leakReport gives FalseSpelling
	end 'satisfying'
end 'satisfyingArgument'

// The accepted predicates as the diagnostics list them — `'os', 'arch', 'testing', 'rcSanitize', or
// 'leakReport'`. Rendered from `allCases` rather than written out, so the sentence a reader is told
// to obey and the set `predicateNamed` actually accepts are the same walk of the same enum.
export function acceptedPredicateList() returns String
	let finalIndex = ConditionPredicate.allCases.count() - 1
	var rendered = ""
	var index = 0

	for predicate in ConditionPredicate.allCases 'each'
		if index > 0 'separator'
			rendered = "{rendered}, "
		end 'separator'
		if index == finalIndex 'lastItem'
			rendered = "{rendered}or "
		end 'lastItem'

		rendered = "{rendered}'{String.from(predicateSpelling(predicate))}'"
		index = index + 1
	end 'each'

	return rendered
end 'acceptedPredicateList'

// The single argument of a predicate call. Accepted kinds are `identifier` (`Windows`, `x64`) and
// the two bool keywords, which are their own kinds in this lexer and would otherwise be rejected as
// "expected identifier" on the very spelling the documentation uses (`testing(true)`).
function readConditionArgument(tokens TokenArray, cursor ConditionCursor) returns Token throws DirectiveError
	let tok = tokenAtOrEof(tokens, index: cursor.value)
	if tok.kind != TokenKind.identifier and tok.kind != TokenKind.true and tok.kind != TokenKind.false 'notAnArgument'
		throw DirectiveError.malformedCondition(tok.line, column: tok.column)
	end 'notAnArgument'
	cursor.value = cursor.value + 1
	return tok
end 'readConditionArgument'

// The token at an absolute index, or a synthetic EOF past the end — the same past-the-end rule
// `Parser.tokenAt` writes down, so a malformed condition that runs off the array reports a position
// instead of faulting. The lexer appends a real EOF, so the synthetic one is a guard.
function tokenAtOrEof(tokens TokenArray, index TokenIndex) returns Token
	if index >= tokens.count() 'pastEnd'
		return Token.eof()
	end 'pastEnd'
	return try tokens.get(index) otherwise panic("tokenAtOrEof: index<count just checked but tokens.get({index}) failed")
end 'tokenAtOrEof'

// Consume a token of `kind` if it is there, reporting whether it was. The optional half of the pair
// below — an `or`/`and`/`not`/`(` that is absent simply ends its production.
function acceptKind(tokens TokenArray, cursor ConditionCursor, kind TokenKind) returns bool
	if tokenAtOrEof(tokens, index: cursor.value).kind != kind 'notThere'
		return false
	end 'notThere'
	cursor.value = cursor.value + 1
	return true
end 'acceptKind'

// Consume a token of `kind` or refuse the condition. The REQUIRED half: a missing `)` or a `#if`
// with no predicate at all has no reading, and the alternative to throwing is resuming the token
// walk at an index the grammar never agreed to.
function expectKind(tokens TokenArray, cursor ConditionCursor, kind TokenKind) returns Token throws DirectiveError
	let tok = tokenAtOrEof(tokens, index: cursor.value)
	if tok.kind != kind 'mismatch'
		throw DirectiveError.malformedCondition(tok.line, column: tok.column)
	end 'mismatch'
	cursor.value = cursor.value + 1
	return tok
end 'expectKind'

// ASCII case-insensitive byte equality. The corpus writes `os(Windows)` and this file's constants are
// lowercase, so every predicate argument compares through here. Compares the SLICES in place — a
// `Token.value` is a zero-copy view of the source buffer (see `Token.value`), and lower-casing either
// side would allocate a String per atom for a handful of byte compares.
function equalsIgnoringAsciiCase(a ByteArray, b ByteArray) returns bool
	if a.count() != b.count() 'lengthDiffers'
		return false
	end 'lengthDiffers'

	var i = 0
	while i < a.count() 'compare'
		let av = try a.get(i) otherwise panic("equalsIgnoringAsciiCase: i<count but a.get({i}) failed")
		let bv = try b.get(i) otherwise panic("equalsIgnoringAsciiCase: i<count but b.get({i}) failed")
		if toAsciiLower(av) != toAsciiLower(bv) 'differs'
			return false
		end 'differs'
		i = i + 1
	end 'compare'
	return true
end 'equalsIgnoringAsciiCase'

// ASCII upper-case to lower-case. Bytes outside `A`-`Z` pass through, so a UTF-8 continuation byte is
// never folded — this is deliberately ASCII-only, because the names it compares (`Windows`, `x64`,
// `true`) are keywords of the directive grammar and not user text.
function toAsciiLower(b Byte) returns Byte
	if b >= AsciiUpperA and b <= AsciiUpperZ 'isUpper'
		return (b + AsciiCaseGap) as Byte
	end 'isUpper'
	return b
end 'toAsciiLower'

let AsciiUpperA = 65 as Byte
let AsciiUpperZ = 90 as Byte
let AsciiCaseGap = 32
